{"precision_recall_curve": "The Precision-recall curve is a graphical representation that plots the precision (positive predictive value) against the recall (sensitivity) for different thresholds. It is used to evaluate the performance of a binary classifier, showing the trade-off between precision and recall for different threshold settings.|Here is another description: The precision-recall curve is a graphical representation that illustrates the trade-off between precision and recall for a predictive model. It is used to evaluate the performance of the model across different thresholds.|Here is another description: The precision-recall curve is a graphical representation used to evaluate the performance of a binary classifier system as the threshold is varied. It plots the precision against the recall, providing insights into the trade-off between the two metrics."},
{"precision_recall_curve__corresponds_to__our_nominal_prediction_model_on_the_test_set": "The phrase \"corresponds to\" indicates a direct relationship or equivalence between the precision-recall curve and the performance metrics of the nominal prediction model when applied to the test set."},
{"our_nominal_prediction_model_on_the_test_set": "The nominal prediction model on the test set refers to the specific implementation of a predictive model that has been trained and is being evaluated using a designated test dataset. The performance of this model is represented by the strong line in the precision-recall curve."},
{"precision_recall_curve__shows__range_between_maximum_and_minimum_precision_for_neural_networks": "The predicate 'shows' indicates that the precision-recall curve is displaying or illustrating a specific aspect or range of data related to the performance of neural networks."},
{"range_between_maximum_and_minimum_precision_for_neural_networks": "The range between maximum and minimum precision for neural networks refers to the variability in precision values that neural networks can achieve when trained with the same configuration but different initializations. This range is visually represented in the shaded region of the precision-recall curve."},
{"neural_networks__are_trained_with__the_same_configuration_and_different_initialization": "The phrase 'are trained with' indicates the process by which neural networks learn from data. Training involves adjusting the weights and biases of the network to minimize the difference between predicted and actual outcomes, using algorithms like backpropagation and optimization techniques."},
{"the_same_configuration_and_different_initialization": "The same configuration and different initialization refers to the setup of neural networks where the architecture, hyperparameters, and training data remain constant, but the initial values of the weights and biases are varied. This approach helps in understanding the impact of initialization on the training process and the final performance of the network."},
{"points": "Points in the context refer to specific data points on the precision-recall curve that represent the performance metrics of different entities."},
{"points__correspond_to__performance_of_resident_medical_doctors_and_students": "The phrase 'correspond to' indicates a relationship where the points on the precision-recall curve are directly associated with or represent the performance metrics of resident medical doctors and students."},
{"performance_of_resident_medical_doctors_and_students": "The performance of resident medical doctors and students, as mentioned in the context, is evaluated based on their ability to diagnose ECG abnormalities, which is compared against a nominal prediction model and a deep neural network (DNN) on the test set."},
{"point": "The 'Point' in the context refers to specific data points plotted on the precision-recall curve, which represent the performance metrics of different models or individuals in diagnosing ECG abnormalities."},
{"point__corresponds_to__dnn_performance_for_the_same_threshold_used_for_generating_table_2": "The predicate 'corresponds to' indicates a direct relationship where the subject (Point) is associated with a specific performance metric or condition, in this case, the DNN performance at a particular threshold."},
{"dnn_performance_for_the_same_threshold_used_for_generating_table_2": "The 'DNN performance for the same threshold used for generating Table 2' refers to the performance metrics of a Deep Neural Network (DNN) at a specific threshold, which is consistent with the threshold used in the creation of Table 2. This performance is compared against other models and human evaluators on the precision-recall curve."},
{"gray_dashed_curves": "The gray dashed curves are visual elements in a precision-recall curve graph, specifically in the background of the figure, used to provide additional context or information about the performance metrics being displayed."},
{"gray_dashed_curves__correspond_to__iso_f1_curves": "The phrase \"correspond to\" indicates a relationship where the gray dashed curves are associated with or represent specific values or conditions, in this case, iso-F1 curves."},
{"iso_f1_curves": "Iso-F1 curves are lines on a precision-recall graph that represent points with a constant F1 score. These curves help in understanding the trade-offs between precision and recall for a given F1 score, providing a visual representation of model performance across different thresholds."},
{"table_2__presents__model": "The predicate \"presents\" indicates that Table 2 serves as a medium to display and organize information about the model, including its performance metrics and comparisons with human annotators."},
{"fig_2__shows__point_corresponding_to_the_maximum_f1_score_for_each_abnormality": "The predicate \"shows\" indicates that Fig. 2 visually presents or illustrates specific information. In this context, it is used to convey that the figure provides a visual representation of the points corresponding to the maximum F1 score for each abnormality detected by the model."},
{"point_corresponding_to_the_maximum_f1_score_for_each_abnormality": "The point corresponding to the maximum F1 score for each abnormality represents the optimal balance between precision and recall for the detection of specific medical conditions by the model. These points are critical as they indicate the threshold at which the model performs best for each type of abnormality, which is then used to generate the DNN scores shown in Table 2."},
