{"noise_and_interference_in_the_baseline": "Noise and interference in the baseline refer to unwanted signals or disturbances that affect the accuracy of electrocardiogram (ECG) readings. These can arise from various sources, including electrical equipment, patient movement, or other environmental factors."},
{"noise_and_interference_in_the_baseline__are__established_causes_of_error": "The phrase \"are established causes of error\" indicates that noise and interference in the baseline have been recognized and documented as significant factors that can lead to inaccuracies in ECG interpretation, both in automatic systems and manual diagnoses by healthcare professionals."},
{"established_causes_of_error": "Established causes of error in the context of ECG analysis refer to well-documented factors that contribute to mistakes or inaccuracies in diagnosing ECG abnormalities. These can include technical issues like noise and interference, as well as human factors such as conceptual or attention errors by medical professionals."},
{"noise_and_interference": "Noise and interference refer to unwanted signals or disturbances that can affect the quality of an electrocardiogram (ECG) recording. These can arise from various sources, such as electrical equipment, patient movement, or muscle activity, and can obscure the true cardiac signal."},
{"noise_and_interference__affected__both_automatic_and_manual_diagnosis_of_ecg_abnormalities": "The term 'affected' in this context signifies that the presence of noise and interference has a detrimental impact on the accuracy and reliability of ECG diagnosis. It implies that these disturbances can lead to misinterpretation of the ECG data, potentially resulting in incorrect diagnoses."},
{"both_automatic_and_manual_diagnosis_of_ecg_abnormalities": "Both automatic and manual diagnosis of ECG abnormalities refers to the processes by which ECGs are analyzed to detect and classify cardiac conditions. Automatic diagnosis involves the use of algorithms and software to interpret ECG data, while manual diagnosis is performed by healthcare professionals who visually inspect and analyze the ECG tracings."},
{"the_dnn__seems_to_be__more_robust_to_noise": "The phrase \"seems to be\" indicates an observation or conclusion drawn from the performance of the DNN in comparison to other methods or human reviewers. It suggests that the DNN exhibits certain characteristics or behaviors under specific conditions."},
{"more_robust_to_noise": "Being \"more robust to noise\" means that the DNN is less affected by errors or inaccuracies caused by noise and interference in the ECG data. This robustness is an important feature for an automatic classification system, as it can lead to more reliable and accurate diagnoses."},
{"the_dnn__made__fewer_mistakes_of_this_type_compared_to_the_medical_residents_and_students": "The predicate \"made\" indicates the action performed by the DNN in comparison to medical residents and students. It suggests that the DNN has been evaluated based on its performance in making diagnoses from ECG data."},
{"fewer_mistakes_of_this_type_compared_to_the_medical_residents_and_students": "Fewer mistakes of this type compared to the medical residents and students refers to the DNN's performance in handling noise and interference in the baseline of ECG readings. Despite the presence of noise, the DNN demonstrated greater robustness and made fewer diagnostic errors than human medical professionals, particularly in scenarios where noise could significantly impact the accuracy of manual diagnosis."},
{"conceptual_errors__were_more_frequent_for__emergency_residents_and_medical_students_than_for_cardiology_residents": "The phrase \"were more frequent for\" indicates a comparative relationship, highlighting that the occurrence of conceptual errors was notably higher in one group compared to another within the medical field."},
{"emergency_residents_and_medical_students_than_for_cardiology_residents": "Emergency residents and medical students represent groups within the medical profession that are in earlier stages of their careers or training. Cardiology residents, on the other hand, are more specialized and experienced in the field of cardiology, having undergone more focused training in heart-related conditions."},
{"attention_errors__were_present_at_a_similar_ratio_for__cardiology_residents_emergency_residents_and_medical_students": "The phrase \"were present at a similar ratio for\" indicates that the occurrence of attention errors was comparable across different groups of medical professionals. This suggests a consistent level of oversight or lack of careful review among these groups when interpreting ECG exams."},
{"cardiology_residents_emergency_residents_and_medical_students": "Cardiology residents, emergency residents, and medical students are groups of medical professionals at various stages of their training. Cardiology residents specialize in cardiovascular medicine, emergency residents work in emergency departments, and medical students are in the early stages of their medical education. Despite their different levels of experience and specialization, they exhibited similar rates of attention errors in ECG interpretation."},
{"the_performance_of_the_emergency_residents": "The performance of emergency residents refers to their ability to accurately interpret and diagnose ECG abnormalities in clinical settings. This performance is crucial as it directly impacts patient care and diagnosis accuracy."},
