{"electrocardiogram_ecg": "The Electrocardiogram (ECG) is a non-invasive diagnostic tool that records the electrical activity of the heart over a period of time using electrodes placed on the skin. It is widely used in clinical settings to detect abnormalities in heart rhythm and to diagnose various heart conditions."},
{"electrocardiogram_ecg__is_a_major_tool_in__diagnoses_of_cardiovascular_diseases": "Being a major tool in the diagnoses of cardiovascular diseases signifies that the Electrocardiogram (ECG) plays a critical role in identifying and assessing heart-related conditions. It is essential for detecting abnormalities and providing valuable information for diagnosis and treatment planning."},
{"diagnoses_of_cardiovascular_diseases": "Diagnoses of cardiovascular diseases refer to the process of identifying and determining the nature and cause of heart-related conditions. This includes a range of disorders affecting the heart and blood vessels, such as coronary artery disease, heart failure, arrhythmias, and valvular heart disease, among others."},
{"ecgs": "ECGs, or electrocardiograms, are diagnostic tools used to assess the electrical activity of the heart. They are crucial in detecting various cardiovascular conditions and are widely used in clinical settings."},
{"ecgs__transitioned_from__analog_to_digital": "The transition from analog to digital refers to the shift in technology used to record and analyze ECGs. This change has enabled more advanced computer analysis and automation in interpreting ECG results."},
{"analog_to_digital": "The transition from analog to digital in ECGs signifies the move from traditional paper-based, manually interpreted ECGs to modern, computer-based systems that allow for automated analysis and storage in digital formats."},
