{"splits__stratified__randomly_by_patient_or_in_chronological_order": "The predicate describes the method by which the ECG records are divided into the aforementioned sets. In this case, the division is done in a stratified manner, which means that the records are divided in a way that maintains the proportion of different categories or characteristics within each set."},
{"randomly_by_patient_or_in_chronological_order": "The object specifies the manner in which the stratification is performed. Here, it is done randomly, by patient, or in chronological order. This means that the records can be divided without any specific order, based on individual patients, or according to the time they were recorded, respectively."},
{"splits__used_for__training_validation_and_a_second_larger_test_set": "The predicate 'used for' indicates the purpose or function of the splits in the context of machine learning model development. It signifies that the splits are employed to facilitate different stages of the model's lifecycle, such as training, validation, and testing."},
{"training_validation_and_a_second_larger_test_set": "The object 'training, validation, and a second larger test set' describes the specific datasets that are created from the splits. These datasets are essential for training the model, validating its performance during development, and finally, testing its effectiveness on new, unseen data."},
{"results": "The results refer to the outcomes of an analysis comparing different models using various data splits for training, validation, and testing.|Here is another description: The results refer to the outcomes of a study evaluating the performance of a deep neural network (DNN) in recognizing ECG abnormalities. These results indicate that the DNN can accurately identify certain ECG abnormalities, performing at least as well as medical residents and students."},
{"results__indicate__no_statistically_significant_difference_between_original_dnn_and_alternative_models": "The predicate \"indicate\" signifies that the results suggest or point towards a specific conclusion or observation about the models being compared."},
{"no_statistically_significant_difference_between_original_dnn_and_alternative_models": "The object \"no statistically significant difference between original DNN and alternative models\" describes the conclusion that there is no meaningful difference in performance between the original deep neural network (DNN) and the alternative models developed using different data splits, except for the model using a chronologically ordered split."},
{"exception": "The exception refers to a specific scenario or case that deviates from the general pattern or rule observed in the study. In this context, it highlights a particular model that does not conform to the overall results."},
{"exception__is__model_developed_using_chronologically_ordered_split": "The predicate \"is\" serves as a linking verb to connect the subject \"exception\" with its description or characteristic. It indicates that the subject possesses a certain quality or attribute, in this case, being the model developed using a chronologically ordered split."},
{"model_developed_using_chronologically_ordered_split": "The model developed using the chronologically ordered split is a specific machine learning model that was trained, validated, and tested using a dataset that was divided based on chronological order. This model is an exception to the general findings of the study, as it was affected by changes over time in the telehealth center operation."},
{"changes_along_time": "The changes along time refer to the temporal variations and developments that occur in the operation of the telehealth center. These changes can include updates in technology, shifts in patient demographics, or modifications in healthcare protocols, all of which evolve over time."},
{"changes_along_time__affected__splits": "The predicate \"affected\" indicates that the changes along time have had an impact or influence on the splits. This means that the temporal changes have caused some form of alteration or consequence on how the data is divided into training, validation, and test sets."},
{"reference": "The reference in the context is a citation or mention of a specific source or document that provides additional information or evidence to support the claims or findings discussed in the text."},
{"reference__cf__supplementary_fig_2": "The predicate \"cf.\" is an abbreviation for the Latin term \"confer,\" which means \"compare\" or \"consult.\" In this context, it is used to direct the reader to compare the information presented in the text with the content of Supplementary Fig. 2 for further understanding or validation of the discussed points."},
{"supplementary_fig_2": "Supplementary Fig. 2 is an additional figure provided alongside the main text, intended to offer more detailed visual information or data that complements the discussion. It is referenced to help readers gain a deeper insight into the topic by comparing it with the information presented in the main text."},
{"this_paper__demonstrates__the_effectiveness_of_end_to_end_automatic_s12l_ecg_classification": "The verb \"demonstrates\" in this context signifies that the paper provides clear evidence or proof of a particular concept or method's efficacy. It implies that the paper includes experimental results, data analysis, or theoretical arguments that support the effectiveness of the discussed method."},
{"the_effectiveness_of_end_to_end_automatic_s12l_ecg_classification": "The effectiveness of \u201cend-to-end\u201d automatic S12L-ECG classification refers to the capability of a machine learning model to accurately classify 12-lead electrocardiogram (ECG) signals in an automated manner, without the need for manual feature extraction. This approach contrasts with traditional methods that require pre-processing of the ECG signals to extract features before classification."},
{"end_to_end_learning": "End-to-end learning is a machine learning approach where the raw input data is directly used to train a model to make predictions, bypassing the need for manual feature extraction.|Here is another description: End-to-end learning is a method where the entire process from input to output is learned by the system itself, without the need for manual feature extraction. In the context of ECG classification, it represents a shift from traditional methods that rely on handcrafted features.|Here is another description: End-to-end learning is a method where the raw signal is directly used as input to a classifier, which autonomously learns to extract features and classify the signal, representing a shift from traditional two-step ECG analysis methods."},
{"end_to_end_learning__presents__an_alternative_to_two_step_approaches": "The term \"presents\" in this context signifies that end-to-end learning offers or introduces a new method or approach as an option or solution."},
{"an_alternative_to_two_step_approaches": "Two-step approaches in ECG analysis involve first extracting features from the ECG signal using traditional signal processing techniques, and then using these features as inputs to a classifier. End-to-end learning provides an alternative by using the raw signal directly as input to the classifier."},
{"classical_methods": "Classical methods refer to traditional approaches used in ECG analysis, which involve a two-step process. These methods typically rely on established algorithms and techniques to analyze ECG signals.|Here is another description: Classical methods refer to traditional approaches in ECG automatic analysis, which involve extracting main features of the ECG signal using traditional signal processing techniques and then using these features as inputs to a classifier."},
{"classical_methods__extract__main_features_of_the_ecg_signal_using_traditional_signal_processing_techniques": "The predicate \"extract\" in this context signifies the action of isolating or deriving specific characteristics or data points from the ECG signal. This extraction is a critical step in preparing the data for subsequent analysis or classification."},
{"main_features_of_the_ecg_signal_using_traditional_signal_processing_techniques": "The main features of the ECG signal are the key characteristics or data points that are derived using traditional signal processing techniques. These features are essential for the subsequent classification of the ECG signal and are typically used as inputs to a classifier in classical ECG analysis methods."},
{"end_to_end_learning__uses__raw_signal_itself_as_an_input_to_the_classifier": "The predicate \"uses\" in this context signifies the utilization of a specific input method by the end-to-end learning approach. It indicates that the method directly employs the raw data without any preprocessing or feature extraction."},
{"raw_signal_itself_as_an_input_to_the_classifier": "The raw signal itself as an input to the classifier refers to the direct use of the original, unprocessed ECG signal data. This approach contrasts with traditional methods that first extract features from the signal before classification."},
{"end_to_end_learning__has_presented__performance_superior_to_commercial_ecg_software_in_an_emergency_room_setting": "The predicate \"has presented\" indicates that end-to-end learning has demonstrated or shown a particular outcome or result in a specific context, in this case, an emergency room setting."},
{"performance_superior_to_commercial_ecg_software_in_an_emergency_room_setting": "Performance superior to commercial ECG software in an emergency room setting refers to the effectiveness and accuracy of end-to-end learning methods in classifying ECG signals, outperforming traditional commercial software that relies on classical signal processing techniques."},
{"neural_networks__have_been_used_for__classification_of_ecgs_in_a_classical_feature_based_setup_and_in_an_end_to_end_learn_setup": "The phrase \"have been used for\" indicates the application or purpose for which neural networks have been employed. In this context, it highlights the versatility of neural networks in addressing different types of classification tasks related to ECGs."},
{"classification_of_ecgs_in_a_classical_feature_based_setup_and_in_an_end_to_end_learn_setup": "Classification of ECGs refers to the process of categorizing electrocardiogram signals into different classes based on their characteristics. This can be done using traditional feature-based methods, where specific features are extracted from the ECG signal and then used for classification, or through end-to-end learning, where the raw ECG signal is directly input into the classifier."},
{"hybrid_methods": "Hybrid methods in the context of ECG classification refer to approaches that integrate traditional signal processing techniques with modern machine learning algorithms. These methods aim to leverage the strengths of both handcrafted features and learned features to improve the accuracy and reliability of ECG analysis."},
{"hybrid_methods__combine__handcrafted_and_learned_features_or_use_a_two_stage_training": "The predicate 'combine' in this context signifies the integration or merging of two distinct methodologies or feature sets within the hybrid methods. This combination aims to enhance the overall performance of ECG classification by utilizing the complementary aspects of handcrafted and learned features."},
{"handcrafted_and_learned_features_or_use_a_two_stage_training": "The object 'handcrafted and learned features or use a two-stage training' describes the specific approaches within hybrid methods. Handcrafted features are manually designed based on domain knowledge, while learned features are automatically extracted by machine learning algorithms. A two-stage training approach involves first training a model to learn features and then using these features to train a classifier."},
{"the_paradigm_shift_towards_end_to_end_learning": "The paradigm shift towards end-to-end learning refers to the transition from traditional two-step ECG analysis methods, which involve feature extraction followed by classification, to a more integrated approach where raw ECG signals are directly input into classifiers that learn to extract features themselves."},
{"the_paradigm_shift_towards_end_to_end_learning__had__a_significant_impact_on_the_size_of_the_datasets_used_for_training_the_models": "The predicate \"had\" indicates a causal relationship where the paradigm shift towards end-to-end learning directly resulted in changes to the methodology and requirements for training models in ECG analysis."},
{"a_significant_impact_on_the_size_of_the_datasets_used_for_training_the_models": "The significant impact on the size of the datasets used for training the models refers to the necessity for larger datasets in end-to-end learning approaches compared to traditional methods, as end-to-end learning models require more data to effectively learn feature extraction and classification from raw signals."},
{"classical_methods__train_their_models_on__datasets_with_few_examples": "The phrase \"train their models on\" indicates the process by which classical methods utilize specific datasets to develop and refine their classification algorithms."},
{"datasets_with_few_examples": "Datasets with few examples, such as the MIT-BIH arrhythmia database with only 47 unique patients, are small-scale collections of ECG data used for training models in classical methods."},
{"end_to_end_deep_learning_or_mixed_approaches": "End-to-end deep learning or mixed approaches refer to methods that either use raw signals directly as inputs to a classifier or combine traditional signal processing techniques with learned features for ECG classification. These methods represent a shift from classical two-step ECG analysis methods."},
{"end_to_end_deep_learning_or_mixed_approaches__have_constructed__large_datasets_ranging_from_3000_to_100_000_unique_patients": "The phrase \"have constructed\" indicates that researchers using end-to-end deep learning or mixed approaches have built and utilized large datasets for training their models. This construction involves gathering and preparing extensive collections of ECG data."},
{"large_datasets_ranging_from_3000_to_100_000_unique_patients": "Large datasets, ranging from 3000 to 100,000 unique patients, are collections of ECG recordings used to train machine learning models. These datasets vary in size and are crucial for improving the performance and reliability of end-to-end deep learning or mixed approach models in ECG classification."},
{"large_datasets_from_previous_work": "Large datasets from previous work refer to extensive collections of data used in prior research studies, particularly in the context of electrocardiogram (ECG) analysis and classification. These datasets are crucial for training and validating machine learning models, especially in the shift towards end-to-end learning approaches."},
{"large_datasets_from_previous_work__were_obtained_from__cardiac_monitors_and_holter_exams_or_consist_of_12_lead_ecgs_obtained_in_an_emergency_room_setting": "The phrase \"were obtained from\" indicates the source or origin of the datasets. It suggests that the data was collected or derived from specific medical monitoring devices or clinical settings, highlighting the diversity in data acquisition methods used in previous research."},
{"cardiac_monitors_and_holter_exams_or_consist_of_12_lead_ecgs_obtained_in_an_emergency_room_setting": "Cardiac monitors and Holter exams are devices used for continuous or periodic monitoring of heart activity, typically recording one or two leads of ECG data over extended periods. On the other hand, 12-lead ECGs obtained in an emergency room setting provide a more comprehensive view of heart activity but are usually recorded over a shorter duration. Both sources contribute to the diversity and size of datasets used in ECG analysis research."},
{"our_dataset": "Our dataset is a large collection of electrocardiogram (ECG) recordings specifically gathered for the purpose of training and testing machine learning models in the field of ECG classification. It is characterized by its extensive size, comprising well over 2 million entries, which significantly surpasses many traditional datasets used in similar research.|Here is another description: Our dataset is a comprehensive collection of electrocardiogram (ECG) tracings that is significantly larger than those used in previous studies. It includes a wide range of ECG abnormalities, from rhythm disorders to conduction disturbances."},
{"our_dataset__consists_of__short_duration_7_10_s_s12l_ecg_tracings_obtained_from_in_clinic_exams": "The phrase \"consists of\" indicates the composition or makeup of the subject, in this case, the dataset. It suggests that the dataset is made up of specific types of ECG tracings, highlighting the nature and characteristics of the data included within it."},
{"short_duration_7_10_s_s12l_ecg_tracings_obtained_from_in_clinic_exams": "The object describes the specific type of ECG tracings that make up the dataset: short duration (7\u221210 s) S12L-ECG tracings obtained from in-clinic exams. These are 12-lead electrocardiogram recordings of brief duration, captured during routine clinical examinations, rather than prolonged monitoring sessions or emergency situations."},
{"orders_of_magnitude": "The subject \"orders of magnitude\" refers to the scale or size of something, often used to describe a significant difference in quantity or degree. In this context, it highlights the substantial increase in the scale of data or parameters used in the current study compared to previous research."},
{"orders_of_magnitude__are__larger_than_those_used_in_previous_studies": "The predicate \"are\" serves as a linking verb that connects the subject \"orders of magnitude\" to the object, indicating that the subject possesses the quality or characteristic described by the object. Here, it establishes that the scale of the current study is significantly larger than that of previous studies."},
{"larger_than_those_used_in_previous_studies": "The object \"larger than those used in previous studies\" describes the extent to which the current study's parameters or data scale surpasses that of earlier research. This suggests that the current study employs a much greater volume or complexity of data, which could lead to more comprehensive and accurate results."},
{"it": "The subject refers to a comprehensive study or dataset that is significantly larger in scale compared to previous research efforts. It is designed to provide a more extensive analysis of electrocardiogram (ECG) abnormalities."},
{"it__encompasses__not_only_rhythm_disorders_like_af_sb_and_st_as_in_previous_studies_15_but_also_conduction_disturbances_such_as_1davb_rbbb_and_lbbb": "The predicate \"encompasses\" indicates that the subject includes a wide range of conditions and abnormalities, going beyond what was covered in earlier studies. It suggests an expanded scope that integrates various types of ECG irregularities."},
{"not_only_rhythm_disorders_like_af_sb_and_st_as_in_previous_studies_15_but_also_conduction_disturbances_such_as_1davb_rbbb_and_lbbb": "The object describes the specific types of ECG abnormalities included in the study, which are not limited to rhythm disorders such as Atrial Fibrillation (AF), Sinus Bradycardia (SB), and Sinus Tachycardia (ST). It also includes conduction disturbances like first-degree Atrioventricular block (1dAVb), Right Bundle Branch Block (RBBB), and Left Bundle Branch Block (LBBB)."},
{"our_dataset__provides__annotation_for_s12l_ecg_exams": "The predicate 'provides' indicates that the dataset offers or supplies specific resources or information. In this context, it means the dataset supplies detailed annotations for the ECG exams."},
{"annotation_for_s12l_ecg_exams": "Annotation for S12L-ECG exams refers to the detailed labeling and interpretation of 12-lead electrocardiogram examinations. These annotations are crucial for diagnosing various cardiac conditions and are the most common type of ECG used in clinical practice."},
{"s12l_ecg_exams__are__the_most_common_in_clinical_practice": "The predicate \"are\" in this context signifies the prevalent status or frequency of S12L-ECG exams in comparison to other types of ECG exams. It highlights their widespread use and importance in everyday clinical practice."},
{"the_most_common_in_clinical_practice": "Being the most common in clinical practice means that S12L-ECG exams are the standard or preferred method for conducting ECGs in hospitals and clinics. Their frequent use is due to their comprehensive diagnostic capabilities and the wide range of heart conditions they can detect."},
{"the_availability_of_such_a_large_database_of_s12l_ecg_tracings": "The availability of a large database of S12L-ECG tracings refers to the extensive collection of electrocardiogram records that are annotated for a wide spectrum of abnormalities, significantly larger than those used in previous studies."},
{"the_availability_of_such_a_large_database_of_s12l_ecg_tracings__opens_up__the_possibility_of_extending_initial_results_of_end_to_end_dnn_in_ecg_automatic_analysis_15_to_a_system_with_applicability_in_a_wide_range_of_clinical_settings": "The phrase \"opens up\" indicates the creation of new opportunities or possibilities. In this context, it suggests that the large database of S12L-ECG tracings creates new opportunities for research and development in the field of ECG automatic analysis."},
{"the_possibility_of_extending_initial_results_of_end_to_end_dnn_in_ecg_automatic_analysis_15_to_a_system_with_applicability_in_a_wide_range_of_clinical_settings": "The possibility of extending initial results of end-to-end DNN in ECG automatic analysis to a system with applicability in a wide range of clinical settings refers to the potential for developing advanced, high-accuracy automatic ECG classification systems that can be used in various medical environments, thereby improving diagnostic processes and patient care."},
{"the_development_of_such_technologies": "The development of such technologies refers to the advancement and creation of new methods and tools, specifically in the field of electrocardiogram (ECG) analysis using deep neural networks (DNN). This involves leveraging large datasets and innovative techniques to improve the accuracy and efficiency of ECG interpretation."},
{"the_development_of_such_technologies__may_yield__high_accuracy_automatic_ecg_classification_systems_that_could_save_clinicians_considerable_time_and_prevent_wrong_diagnoses": "May yield indicates a potential outcome or result that could be achieved through the application or implementation of the developed technologies. In this context, it suggests that the advancements in DNN for ECG analysis have the possibility to produce significant benefits."},
{"high_accuracy_automatic_ecg_classification_systems_that_could_save_clinicians_considerable_time_and_prevent_wrong_diagnoses": "High-accuracy automatic ECG classification systems that could save clinicians considerable time and prevent wrong diagnoses are advanced technological solutions designed to automatically analyze and classify ECG data with a high degree of accuracy. These systems aim to assist clinicians by reducing the time required for manual interpretation and minimizing the occurrence of incorrect diagnoses, thereby enhancing patient care and outcomes."},
{"millions_of_s12l_ecgs": "Millions of S12L-ECGs refer to a large number of standard 12-lead electrocardiogram tests conducted annually. These tests are crucial for diagnosing a wide range of cardiovascular conditions and are commonly used in clinical practice."},
{"millions_of_s12l_ecgs__are_performed__every_year": "The predicate \"are performed\" indicates the action of conducting or carrying out the S12L-ECG tests. This action is a routine procedure in medical settings to assess heart activity and detect abnormalities."},
{"every_year": "\"Every year\" signifies the frequency with which the S12L-ECGs are conducted. This high annual volume underscores the importance and widespread use of these tests in diagnosing and monitoring heart conditions across various clinical settings."},
{"an_accurate_classification_system": "An accurate classification system refers to a sophisticated method or technology designed to categorize and interpret data with high precision. In the context of medical diagnostics, particularly for cardiovascular diseases, such a system would involve analyzing electrocardiogram (ECG) data to identify various heart conditions accurately."},
{"an_accurate_classification_system__could_help__to_detect_wrong_diagnoses_and_improve_the_access_of_patients_from_deprived_and_remote_locations_to_this_essential_diagnostic_tool_of_cardiovascular_diseases": "The phrase \"could help\" indicates a potential beneficial impact or assistance provided by the subject. In this case, it suggests that an accurate classification system has the capability to offer significant advantages in the medical field, specifically in improving diagnostic processes."},
{"to_detect_wrong_diagnoses_and_improve_the_access_of_patients_from_deprived_and_remote_locations_to_this_essential_diagnostic_tool_of_cardiovascular_diseases": "Detecting wrong diagnoses and improving access for patients from deprived and remote locations to essential diagnostic tools for cardiovascular diseases involves enhancing the accuracy of medical diagnoses and ensuring that patients, regardless of their geographic or socioeconomic status, have access to critical healthcare resources. This includes the use of advanced diagnostic tools like ECGs to identify and treat heart conditions effectively."},
{"the_error_analysis": "The error analysis refers to a detailed examination of the mistakes made by a deep neural network (DNN) in the context of electrocardiogram (ECG) interpretation. This analysis is crucial for understanding the limitations and potential improvements needed for the DNN to enhance its performance in clinical settings.|Here is another description: The error analysis refers to the process of examining and evaluating the mistakes or inaccuracies that occurred within the study, particularly in relation to the datasets and the deep neural network implementation."},
{"the_error_analysis__shows__that_most_of_the_dnn_mistakes_were_related_to_measurements_of_ecg_intervals": "The phrase \"shows\" indicates the revelation or demonstration of specific findings from the error analysis. In this context, it highlights the primary areas where the DNN encountered difficulties, providing insights into the nature of its errors."},
{"that_most_of_the_dnn_mistakes_were_related_to_measurements_of_ecg_intervals": "The object \"that most of the DNN mistakes were related to measurements of ECG intervals\" describes the specific type of errors identified in the analysis. These errors involve the DNN's inaccuracies in measuring ECG intervals, which are critical for diagnosing various heart conditions. The mistakes often occurred in borderline cases where precise measurements are essential for correct diagnosis."},
{"most_of_those": "The subject refers to the majority of errors made by the deep neural network (DNN) in the context of ECG analysis. These errors are significant as they impact the accuracy of automatic ECG classification systems, which are crucial for clinical settings, especially in areas with a shortage of qualified medical professionals."},
{"most_of_those__were__borderline_cases_where_the_diagnosis_relies_on_a_consensus_definitions_39_that_can_only_be_ascertained_when_a_measurement_is_above_a_sharp_cutoff_point": "The predicate indicates that the majority of the DNN's errors were associated with specific types of cases. These cases are critical because they influence the overall performance and reliability of the DNN in diagnosing ECG abnormalities."},
{"borderline_cases_where_the_diagnosis_relies_on_a_consensus_definitions_39_that_can_only_be_ascertained_when_a_measurement_is_above_a_sharp_cutoff_point": "The object describes borderline cases in ECG diagnosis where the determination of an abnormality depends on consensus definitions and specific measurement thresholds. These cases are challenging for the DNN because they require precise encoding of sharp cutoff points, which the DNN may fail to accurately capture, leading to misclassifications."},
{"the_mistakes": "The mistakes refer to errors made by the deep neural network (DNN) in the classification of ECG abnormalities, particularly in measurements of ECG intervals that are borderline cases."},
{"the_mistakes__can_be_explained_by__the_dnn_failing_to_encode_these_very_sharp_thresholds": "The phrase \"can be explained by\" indicates a causal relationship where the subject (the mistakes) is caused or influenced by the object (the DNN failing to encode very sharp thresholds)."},
{"the_dnn_failing_to_encode_these_very_sharp_thresholds": "The DNN failing to encode these very sharp thresholds refers to the deep neural network's inability to accurately represent or learn the precise cutoff points that are critical for diagnosing certain ECG abnormalities, leading to errors in classification."},
{"supplementary_fig_3": "Supplementary Fig. 3 is a visual representation included in the document to provide additional information or clarification on a specific topic discussed in the text."},
{"supplementary_fig_3__illustrates__this_effect": "The predicate \"illustrates\" indicates that Supplementary Fig. 3 serves as an example or demonstration of a particular concept, phenomenon, or effect mentioned in the context."},
{"this_effect": "\"This effect\" refers to a specific phenomenon or outcome that is being demonstrated or explained through Supplementary Fig. 3. In this context, it likely relates to the performance of deep neural networks (DNN) in ECG automatic analysis and the types of errors they may encounter."},
{"noise_and_interference_in_the_baseline": "Noise and interference in the baseline refer to unwanted signals or disturbances that can affect the accuracy of ECG readings. These can arise from various sources such as electrical equipment, patient movement, or other environmental factors."},
{"noise_and_interference_in_the_baseline__are__established_causes_of_error_40_and_affected_both_automatic_and_manual_diagnosis_of_ecg_abnormalities": "The phrase \"are established causes of error\" indicates that noise and interference in the baseline have been recognized and documented as significant factors that can lead to inaccuracies in both automatic and manual diagnosis of ECG abnormalities."},
{"established_causes_of_error_40_and_affected_both_automatic_and_manual_diagnosis_of_ecg_abnormalities": "ECG abnormalities refer to deviations from the normal electrical activity of the heart as recorded by an electrocardiogram. These abnormalities can indicate various heart conditions and are critical for accurate diagnosis and treatment. Both automatic systems and manual reviews by medical professionals can be affected by noise and interference, leading to potential misdiagnosis."},
{"the_dnn": "The DNN refers to a deep neural network utilized for the automatic analysis of electrocardiograms (ECGs). It is designed to classify ECG exams, which are critical for diagnosing various heart conditions."},
{"the_dnn__seems_to_be__more_robust_to_noise_and_it_made_fewer_mistakes_of_this_type_compared_to_the_medical_residents_and_students": "The phrase \"seems to be\" indicates an observation or conclusion drawn from the performance of the DNN in handling ECG data, particularly in comparison to human medical professionals."},
{"more_robust_to_noise_and_it_made_fewer_mistakes_of_this_type_compared_to_the_medical_residents_and_students": "The object describes the DNN's performance characteristics, specifically its robustness to noise in ECG data and its lower error rate in certain types of mistakes compared to medical residents and students. This suggests that the DNN is more reliable in noisy conditions and makes fewer errors that are typically made by less experienced human reviewers."},
{"conceptual_errors__were_more_frequent_for__emergency_residents_and_medical_students_than_for_cardiology_residents": "The phrase \"were more frequent for\" indicates a comparative relationship where the occurrence of conceptual errors is higher in one group compared to another. This suggests a disparity in the frequency of these errors among different categories of medical professionals."},
{"emergency_residents_and_medical_students_than_for_cardiology_residents": "Emergency residents and medical students represent two groups of less experienced medical professionals. Emergency residents are doctors in training who work in emergency settings, while medical students are individuals currently pursuing a medical degree. Cardiology residents, on the other hand, are doctors in training who specialize in cardiology and have more focused experience in interpreting ECGs."},
{"attention_errors__were_present_at_a_similar_ratio_for__cardiology_residents_emergency_residents_and_medical_students": "The phrase \"were present at a similar ratio for\" indicates that the occurrence or frequency of attention errors was comparable among different groups of medical professionals."},
{"cardiology_residents_emergency_residents_and_medical_students": "Cardiology residents, emergency residents, and medical students are groups of medical professionals at different stages of their training and specialization. Cardiology residents specialize in cardiovascular medicine, emergency residents work in emergency departments, and medical students are in the process of obtaining their medical degrees."},
{"the_performance_of_the_emergency_residents": "The performance of emergency residents refers to their effectiveness and accuracy in interpreting and diagnosing ECG abnormalities, which is a critical skill in emergency medical settings."},
{"the_performance_of_the_emergency_residents__is_worse_than__the_medical_students_for_many_abnormalities": "The phrase \"is worse than\" indicates a comparative relationship where the performance or outcomes of one group (emergency residents) are inferior to those of another group (medical students) in the context of diagnosing ECG abnormalities."},
{"the_medical_students_for_many_abnormalities": "The medical students for many abnormalities represent a group of individuals who are in the early stages of their medical education but have recently studied the concepts related to ECG interpretation, which may give them an advantage in recalling and applying this knowledge."},
{"our_work": "Our work refers to the research and development efforts in creating a deep neural network (DNN) for automatic ECG classification, which aims to improve the accuracy and efficiency of diagnosing cardiovascular diseases through S12L-ECG exams."},
{"our_work__is_perhaps_best_understood_in_the_context_of__its_limitations": "The phrase \"is perhaps best understood in the context of\" suggests that the true value and implications of the work can be better appreciated by considering the constraints, challenges, and boundaries within which the research was conducted."},
{"its_limitations": "The limitations of the work encompass various factors such as the DNN's difficulty in encoding sharp diagnostic thresholds, susceptibility to noise and interference, and the presence of conceptual and attention errors among medical professionals involved in the study."},
{"we__obtained__the_highest_f1_scores_for_the_dnn": "The predicate \"obtained\" indicates that the subject achieved or attained a certain result or performance metric in their research."},
{"the_highest_f1_scores_for_the_dnn": "The object \"the highest F1 scores for the DNN\" refers to the performance metric achieved by the deep neural network in the study, indicating that it performed better than other methods or previous iterations in terms of the F1 score, a measure of a model's accuracy on a dataset that is balanced between precision and recall."},
